        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "The transpose $\\mathbf{A}^{\\top}$ of a matrix $\\mathbf{A}=[a_{j k}]\\mathrm{~i s~}\\mathbf{A}^{\\mathsf{T}}=[a_{j k}];$ roWS become columns and conversely r (Sec. 7.2). Here, A need not be square. If it is and $\\mathbf{A}=\\mathbf{A}^{\\mathsf{T}},$ then A is called symmetric;if $\\mathbf{A}=-\\mathbf{A}^{\\mathsf{T}},$ it is called skew-symmetric.\nFor a product,$(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{B})^{\\mathsf{T}}=\\mathbf{B}^{\\mathsf{T}}\\mathbf{A}^{\\mathsf{T}}(\\mathrm{S e c.}~7.2).$ \nA main application of matrices concerns l linear systems of equations \n(2)$\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ (Sec. 7.3)(m equations in n unknowns $x_{1},\\cdots,x_{n};$ A and b given).",
